  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Grain Processing**\n\nI\'ve located the grain processing within `SamsungLogColorScience.kt`, specifically lines 420-427, where a conditional statement checks `settings.effects.grainIntensity`. I\'m now digging into the associated code block to understand the implementation details of this \"pseudo-random noise\" and how it\'s integrated with the broader color science. I\'m focusing on the algorithmic approach used to generate and apply the grain effect.\n\n\n**Defining Grain Parameters**\n\nI\'ve examined the noise generation within the codebase and have identified it as quite basic. The user\'s prompt suggests a comprehensive film grain processor, which has prompted a rethinking of current implementations. My thoughts are gravitating towards a **FilmGrainProcessor** class, with parameters for grain strength, size, and roughness, addressing the need for organic texture overlays. This will enable users to fine-tune the grain\'s appearance and complexity.\n\n\n**Refining Grain Characteristics**\n\nI\'m now diving into the granular control parameters. I\'m considering `grainColorType`, differentiating between monochrome and color dye simulations, and incorporating `shadowsAmount`, `midtonesAmount`, and `highlightsAmount` for authentic silver halide density. I also am developing presets reflecting cinema formats, with `35mm Fine Grain` and `16mm Indie Gritty`, among others, and a `Custom` option for user flexibility.\n\n\n**Implementing Grain Algorithms**\n\nI\'m now integrating sophisticated noise algorithms. I\'m focusing on fractal Brownian motion with octave blending for organic roughness, alongside an emulsion response curve, to model grain density correctly. The rendering performance is also essential, so I\'m investigating pre-generated noise tables or efficient generators. I\'m also considering a dedicated UI element, possibly a \"Film Grain Processor\" deck for comprehensive control.\n\n\n**Designing Grain Interface**\n\nI am now designing the user interface, incorporating preset grain styles for cinema formats such as `35mm Fine`, `16mm Indie`, `8mm Vintage`, `65mm IMAX`, and also allowing custom grain configurations. I\'ve included strength, size, and roughness sliders. There is also an emulsion tonal balance control to emphasize midtones or shadows. I also designed a monochrome/color dye switch, and I am working on the live preview for the organic grain structure.\n\n\n"
