  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Assessing Existing Capabilities**\n\nI\'m impressed; the math pipeline is robust! I\'m now comparing its functionality against the user\'s specific requests. I need to determine gaps in color grading options relative to DaVinci Resolve Studio and confirm if the necessary brightness, contrast, and temperature controls exist or need to be incorporated.\n\n\n**Expanding Feature Set**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating primary adjustment sliders within the color grading interface, specifically mirroring DaVinci Resolve Studio\'s functionality. I\'m prioritizing brightness, contrast, temperature, tint, saturation, shadows, highlights, midtone detail, and color boost. I intend to provide a clean and professional UI for these controls. I\'ll need a way to translate wide gamut to rec 709, like the color transform node. I\'m also planning to make the app publicly accessible for testing.\n\n\n**Implementing Primary Sliders**\n\nI\'ve successfully integrated the seven primary adjustment sliders, including numeric readouts, reset buttons, and distinct accent colors. These sliders will mirror DaVinci Resolve\'s functionality. I\'ve also begun to explore the color space transform node, mirroring DaVinci\'s workflow, and this will be important for compatibility.\n\n\n**Defining Color Space Transforms**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of color space transforms (CSTs). I am prioritizing the Output Node, which will use CSTs to map intermediate working color spaces to desired output formats. I\'m focusing on defining input and output color spaces, gamma settings, and tone mapping methods. The goal is to provide a versatile system supporting popular color spaces like DWG, Rec.709, and Rec.2020.\n\n\n**Refining CST Implementation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specifics of the Color Space Transform (CST) node. My priority is to mirror DaVinci Resolve\'s node graph structure, including bypass toggles for quick A/B comparisons. I\'m focusing on defining crucial parameters like tone mapping thresholds, gamut rolloff, and saturation limits to allow for complex color conversions. I\'m also planning to incorporate comprehensive CST parameters, including input/output color spaces and gamma settings.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Node Graph**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the importance of mirroring DaVinci Resolve\'s node graph structure, which is central to a professional color grading workflow. I\'m focusing on incorporating a bypass toggle for the CST node for quick comparisons. Furthermore, I\'m working to integrate a dedicated tab or section for nodes and CST specifically, mirroring DaVinci\'s hallmark feature. I\'ve also identified specific color space transforms for DWG, Rec.2020, and DCI-P3 to Rec.709 conversions.\n\n\n**Exploring Additional Features**\n\nI\'m now expanding my focus to include looks, presets, masks, effects, and other missing features. I\'m taking stock of the remaining options to match DaVinci Resolve Studio\'s color grading capabilities. This will include evaluating and integrating presets and LUTs, and developing masking tools for targeted adjustments and visual effects. I will be working on additional effects as well.\n\n\n**Visualizing Signal Flow**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a dynamic node graph visualizer. I envision a clear signal flow representation (Input -> Primaries -> Curves -> FX -> CST Output Node) with an active node indicator and bypass toggles. I\'m prioritizing adding Resolve-grade broadcast graticules to the RGB Parade and Vectorscope displays, along with implementing a 100% and 75% scale option. I\'ve also identified a one-tap toggle for False Color exposure metering, mirroring DaVinci\'s IRE zones (Purple, Blue, Green/Pink, Yellow, Red). Finally, I\'m integrating a split-screen comparison mode (Wipe / Side-by-Side / Difference) to compare graded and ungraded versions, or different looks.\n\n\n**Generating APK Builds**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the debug APK (`app-debug.apk`) can be built within the Linux container using `gradle assembleDebug`, making it ready for download. I\'m now focusing on a clear, step-by-step explanation for the user on how they can download and install the APK on their Samsung S24 Ultra, through the AI Studio settings menu.\n\n\n**Building APK Download**\n\nI\'ve confirmed I can build the debug APK using `gradle assembleDebug` within the Linux container. I\'m focusing on providing the user with concise instructions for downloading and installing this APK on their Samsung S24 Ultra. I intend to detail this clearly, either by downloading the APK (via the AI Studio settings menu or the Shared App URL). I\'m also implementing the primary adjustment sliders now.\n\n\n**Constructing Node Panels**\n\nI\'m now building the `CstNodePanel.kt` file. I\'m focusing on a visual DaVinci-style node graph with active statuses and bypass toggles for serial nodes. I\'m prioritizing implementing configuration controls for input/output color spaces, gamma settings, tone mapping types, and gamut mapping. I will also incorporate live signal flow description badges for a clear workflow.\n\n\n**Implementing Slider Components**\n\nI\'m developing the `PrimarySlidersDeck.kt` file, aiming for DaVinci Resolve-style slider rows. These will include controls for brightness, contrast, temperature, tint, saturation, shadows, highlights, and color boost, all with appropriate ranges and individual reset buttons. I\'m integrating them directly under the color wheels. Also, I\'m working to integrate a False Color broadcast exposure monitor with IRE zones and an A/B split-screen wipe.\n\n\n**Defining Node Configurations**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining the `CstNodeSettings.kt` data class and its related enums. I\'m prioritizing `InputColorSpace`, `InputGamma`, and `OutputColorSpace` to create a robust model. The goal is to provide multiple options for the Samsung S24 Ultra. I intend to provide a DaVinci-like setup, enabling precise color transformations.\n\n\n\n**Modeling CST Node Parameters**\n\nI\'m now building out the `CstNodeSettings` data class and corresponding enums. I\'m focusing on incorporating options for input/output color spaces, gamma settings, tone mapping methods, and gamut mapping. I\'m defining parameters for a DaVinci-like setup. I\'m working to expand the presets for Samsung S24 Ultra compatibility.\n\n\n\n"
