  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Implementing Color Transformations**\n\nI\'m currently integrating the Color Space Transform (CST) method and False Color visualization into `SamsungLogColorScience.kt`. The goal is to facilitate seamless mapping from wide-gamut log signals like DWG and BT.2020 to various delivery spaces such as Rec.709. The False Color implementation aims for clear signal level visualization.\n\n\n**Refining Color Space Conversion**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific color space conversions within the `applyColorSpaceTransform` function. I\'ve implemented the chromatic adaptation matrices for both DaVinci Wide Gamut and Samsung S24 Gamut to Rec.709. These matrices handle the initial color gamut transformations. The method then proceeds to apply further color grading based on the provided settings.\n\n\n**Converting Color Spaces Now**\n\nI\'ve added specific color space transformations within the existing `applyColorSpaceTransform` function. I\'ve now introduced matrices to handle conversions to Rec.709 from ARRI Wide Gamut 4, Sony SGamut3, and Rec.709. These transformations are integrated as part of the overall color pipeline. After this, a retargeting to DCI-P3, if the target is not Rec.709, will be applied.\n\n\n**Implementing Color Transformations**\n\nI\'ve recently added specific color space transformations to accommodate DCI-P3 and Rec.2020 outputs within the color pipeline. Specifically, conversion matrices have been implemented for Rec.709 to DCI-P3 and Rec.709 to Rec.2020. I\'m also now implementing DaVinci tone mapping.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Tone Mapping Logic**\n\nI\'m currently evaluating different tone mapping implementations within the `SamsungLogColorScience.kt` module. I\'m experimenting with knee compression, luminance-only compression, and off modes to handle potential signal clipping. I\'ve been focusing on optimizing the knee compression method and the luminance-only method for accurate handling of highlights.\n\n\n**Refining Gamut Mapping**\n\nI\'ve just added DaVinci\'s saturation compression gamut mapping as a new feature within the existing color transformation process. I\'m focusing on handling out-of-gamut colors to ensure a smooth transition and maintain luminance, which helps prevent clipping in the output. I\'m also now implementing the final output gamma encoding.\n\n\n**Encoding Output Gamma**\n\nI\'ve just implemented the output gamma encoding logic. I\'ve integrated handling for Gamma 2.2, Rec.709, and sRGB, ensuring that color data is correctly transformed to the appropriate output space. The encoding methods include pow operations and the standard Rec.709 OETF piecewise curve to handle different standards and prevent clipping.\n\n\n**Defining False Color Ranges**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `evaluateFalseColor` function. I\'ve defined color ranges based on IRE values, mapping specific colors to represent signal levels. The range starts from crushed blacks represented in purple, shadows in blue, and low-mids in dark cyan. The IRE ranges also cover middle gray, skin tones in pink, neutral gray, and specular highlights in yellow, and blown highlights in red. This should give a clear visualization of the signal.\n\n\n"
