    thinking: "**Examining Fuji Film Recipes**\n\nI\'m currently looking into the `LutCategory` and `LutPreset` data to identify the seven most well-known Fujifilm recipes. My goal is to determine the best options to build a LUT for the Samsung S24 Ultra, based on user demand for Fujifilm emulation. So far, \"Fujifilm Classic Chrome,\" known for its documentary style, is the first recipe I\'ve found.\n\n\n**Analyzing Fujifilm Recipes Further**\n\nNow, I\'ve identified six additional Fujifilm recipes to emulate. These include \"Classic Negative\" (Superia 400), \"Nostalgic Negative\" (1970s look), \"Velvia 50 / Vivid\" (landscape), \"Provia 100F / Standard\" (neutral palette), \"Astia / Soft\" (portrait), and \"ACROS + Red Filter\" (monochrome). I am moving forward with the goal of creating LUTs for the S24 Ultra, based on user demand for Fujifilm emulation, and these are all of the original recipes to be included.\n\n\n**Implementing Fujifilm Recipes Categorization**\n\nI\'ve decided to add a dedicated `FUJIFILM_RECIPES` category to `LutCategory`, which should make navigation easier. I am moving forward with this to make the LUTs more user friendly. I plan to add the Fujifilm presets into `LutPreset`, now that the foundation is in place. Next, I am going to examine how Bezier Masking works in DaVinci Resolve and how to build an interactive Bezier tool for Android Compose.\n\nI\'m now looking into how Bezier Masking works in DaVinci Resolve, focusing on the custom Bezier Curve. User interaction centers around anchor points and Bezier handles to control the curve\'s shape using cubic Bezier curves.\n\n\n"
