  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Bezier Evaluation**\n\nOkay, I\'ve integrated `evaluateBezierMask(u, v, mask)` into the existing system, placing it strategically before `evaluateMask`, and I\'m calling it appropriately for custom Bezier masks. My focus now is designing the `evaluateBezierMask` function, specifically for cubic Bezier power window masks.\n\n\n**Implementing Bezier Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation of `evaluateBezierMask`. I\'m focusing on the details of calculating the point-in-polygon winding using the sampled Bezier spline. I am also working on applying continuous Hermite smoothstep feathering across the curve boundaries. I\'m tackling the sampling rate to create the Bezier segment polygon to determine winding. I\'m aiming for accuracy in the evaluation.\n\n\n**Calculating Bezier Points**\n\nI\'ve just finished implementing the Bezier curve point calculation, handling the cubic Bezier equation. I\'m focusing on ray-casting now, to determine if a point is inside the Bezier polygon. I\'m also preparing for calculating the shortest distance to the curve boundary.\n\n\n**Determining Mask Distance**\n\nI\'ve been working on determining the shortest distance from a point (u, v) to the Bezier polygon boundary. I iterate through the polygon edges, calculating the squared distance to each. The core of this involves projecting the point onto each line segment and calculating the distance from the point to that projection. I\'m using this distance to calculate a signed distance, indicating whether the point is inside or outside the polygon. Then, I calculate a Hermite smoothstep feathering.\n\n\n**Analyzing Color Transformations**\n\nI\'ve shifted my attention to `SamsungLogColorScience.kt`, specifically examining how LUT presets like Fuji Classic Chrome are implemented. I\'m unpacking the calculations for luminosity, saturation adjustments, and contrast curves to understand the transformations in color spaces. Now I\'m working on how this can integrate with the Bezier evaluation.\n\n\n**Integrating Color Changes**\n\nI\'m now integrating the understanding of the Classic Negative LUT into the Bezier evaluation, specifically considering how color transformations interact with mask application. I\'m focusing on the luminosity, saturation, and contrast adjustments within this LUT and planning how to seamlessly combine these color changes with Bezier feathering. I\'m implementing shadow and highlight adjustments.\n\n\n**Applying Color Formulas**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on implementing the color formulas from the LUT presets, such as Fuji Nostalgic Negative. I\'m focusing on mapping the luminosity calculations to the color components, and applying these to the Bezier evaluation process. I am specifically working to incorporate the Nostalgic Negative\'s amber glow effect. I am also working on the Velvia preset, and the dynamic punch they provide.\n\n\n**Implementing Color LUTs**\n\nI\'m now implementing color transformations for specific LUT presets. I\'ve focused on `FUJI_VELVIA` and I am working through the punchy S-curve and saturation boost to apply it correctly. I\'m also examining the luminosity calculations and how to map these transformations to RGB components. I am using the existing Bezier framework, and I am incorporating these color changes directly.\n\n\n**Implementing Color Curves**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating color curves from various LUT presets, starting with Fuji Astia. I\'m focusing on the soft S-curve to reduce contrast and the skin bias for a warm tone. I am examining how to integrate these transformations to complement the existing color adjustments, and apply them dynamically.\n\n\n"
