  planner_response {
    response: "**No, simply pushing code to GitHub will not automatically update Google Play on its own**, but updating your app on your phone is very simple.\n\nHere is exactly how updates work:\n\n---\n\n### How to push an update to your phone\n\n1. **Make changes in AI Studio**:\n   - Whenever you add a new feature, tweak color math, or adjust the UI, the project builds in AI Studio.\n2. **Push the update via the \"Publish\" button**:\n   - Pushing to GitHub only backs up your code in your GitHub repository.\n   - To send the new version to your phone, go back to the **Publish** menu in AI Studio and click to publish the new update.\n   - AI Studio automatically packages the latest build and uploads the new version to your Google Play personal library.\n3. **On your phone**:\n   - Open the **Google Play Store** on your phone.\n   - Tap your profile icon $\\rightarrow$ **Manage apps & device**.\n   - You will see **ResolveStudio** listed under *Updates available* with an **\"Update\"** button (or Play Store will auto-update it in the background).\n   - Tap **Update** \342\200\224 your app updates seamlessly while keeping all your custom presets, masks, and LUT configurations intact!\n\n---\n\n### What does the \"GitHub\" button do vs the \"Publish\" button?\n\n* **GitHub button**: Syncs your Kotlin and Gradle source code to your GitHub repo so you have your code backed up and version-controlled.\n* **Publish button**: Compiles and sends the runnable app directly to your **Google Play Store library** on your phone.\n\nWhenever you want the latest version on your phone, trigger it from the **Publish** menu in AI Studio."
    thinking: "**Clarifying App Updates**\n\nI\'m focusing on clarifying how updates function in this workflow. I\'m considering the process from pushing changes to GitHub all the way through to testing the updated app on the Play Store. My goal is to outline the steps involved and provide a clear explanation for seamless understanding.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "**No, simply pushing code to GitHub will not automatically update Google Play on its own**, but updating your app on your phone is very simple.\n\nHere is exactly how updates work:\n\n---\n\n### How to push an update to your phone\n\n1. **Make changes in AI Studio**:\n   - Whenever you add a new feature, tweak color math, or adjust the UI, the project builds in AI Studio.\n2. **Push the update via the \"Publish\" button**:\n   - Pushing to GitHub only backs up your code in your GitHub repository.\n   - To send the new version to your phone, go back to the **Publish** menu in AI Studio and click to publish the new update.\n   - AI Studio automatically packages the latest build and uploads the new version to your Google Play personal library.\n3. **On your phone**:\n   - Open the **Google Play Store** on your phone.\n   - Tap your profile icon $\\rightarrow$ **Manage apps & device**.\n   - You will see **ResolveStudio** listed under *Updates available* with an **\"Update\"** button (or Play Store will auto-update it in the background).\n   - Tap **Update** \342\200\224 your app updates seamlessly while keeping all your custom presets, masks, and LUT configurations intact!\n\n---\n\n### What does the \"GitHub\" button do vs the \"Publish\" button?\n\n* **GitHub button**: Syncs your Kotlin and Gradle source code to your GitHub repo so you have your code backed up and version-controlled.\n* **Publish button**: Compiles and sends the runnable app directly to your **Google Play Store library** on your phone.\n\nWhenever you want the latest version on your phone, trigger it from the **Publish** menu in AI Studio."
    thinking_duration {
